{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "toolu_01P2J4HKTK25FhvuFFPC6xuZ", "type": "tool_result", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\"excerpt_removed\": true, \"references\": [{\"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Textile_arts_of_the_Indigenous_peoples_of_the_Americas\"}, {\"url\": \"https://www.textilescusco.org/andean-textiles\"}, {\"url\": \"https://scholarlypublications.universiteitleiden.nl/access/item%3A2878422/view\"}, {\"url\": \"https://www.worldhistory.org/audioplayer/en/2-791/\"}, {\"url\": \"https://82nd-and-fifth.metmuseum.org/toah/hd/adtx/hd_adtx.htm\"}, {\"url\": \"https://scalar.usc.edu/works/andean-tapestry/inca-cumbi\"}, {\"url\": \"https://www.worldhistory.org/article/791/inca-textiles/.md\"}, {\"url\": \"https://brewminate.com/?p=110375\"}, {\"url\": \"https://www.worldhistory.org/audioplayer/fr/2-791/\"}, {\"url\": \"https://en.Wikipedia.com/wiki/Cumbi\"}]}"}]}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-20T23:44:53.927Z", "tool_use_result": {"query": "Inca textiles typical cloth of lower classes highland Andes cumbi awasca", "results": [{"tool_use_id": "srvtoolu_01XNpoeYKC44fc9Gp2fQeZY4", "content": [{"title": "Textile arts of the Indigenous peoples of the Americas", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Textile_arts_of_the_Indigenous_peoples_of_the_Americas"}, {"title": "andean textiles", "url": "https://www.textilescusco.org/andean-textiles"}, {"title": "scholarlypublications.universiteitleiden.nl", "url": "https://scholarlypublications.universiteitleiden.nl/access/item%3A2878422/view"}, {"title": "www.worldhistory.org", "url": "https://www.worldhistory.org/audioplayer/en/2-791/"}, {"title": "hd adtx", "url": "https://82nd-and-fifth.metmuseum.org/toah/hd/adtx/hd_adtx.htm"}, {"title": "Inca Cumbi: Precolumbian Tapestry Traditions", "url": "https://scalar.usc.edu/works/andean-tapestry/inca-cumbi"}, {"title": "www.worldhistory.org", "url": "https://www.worldhistory.org/article/791/inca-textiles/.md"}, {"title": "brewminate.com", "url": "https://brewminate.com/?p=110375"}, {"title": "www.worldhistory.org", "url": "https://www.worldhistory.org/audioplayer/fr/2-791/"}, {"title": "en.wikipedia.com", "url": "https://en.Wikipedia.com/wiki/Cumbi"}]}, "The search results agree that awasca was the everyday cloth of the lower classes, while cumbi was reserved for elites.\n\n- **Awasca:** One source describes it as thick garments worn as standard among highland lower classes, with lighter cotton clothing on the coast. Another calls it the most common cloth, used for daily life and the military but rarely decorative. Sources differ on details: one says it was a coarse sheep or llama wool used for blankets and rugs, while another assigns blankets to the coarser chusi.\n- **Cumbi (qompi):** Its fine tapestry was made from alpaca, baby alpaca, and vicuña fiber. Sources say it was limited to the emperor, nobles, and religious use, and was produced by specialist weavers in state workshops.\n- **Production:** Crude garments were made at the household level, while specialists wove the finer cloth. One source says textile labor was a major form of state tax.\n\nI did not run a second search, so I have not checked the specific thread-count figures or the origin of the awasca name beyond these sources."], "durationSeconds": 5.534529, "searchCount": 1}, "tool_result_meta": [{"id": "toolu_01P2J4HKTK25FhvuFFPC6xuZ", "permission_decision": {"decision": "accept", "source": "hook", "reason_type": "hook"}}]}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgENugDxNKCuMq2bx44Hq", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "signature": ""}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 1039, "cache_read_input_tokens": 4313, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 1039}, "output_tokens": 3, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-20T23:45:01.545Z", "request_id": "req_011CgENufuc519SXg8pARBPP", "thinking_duration_ms": 6444, "thinking_display": "updates"}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgENugDxNKCuMq2bx44Hq", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "**答案:awasca(也拼作 awasqa)。** 这是一种粗织的日常布料,印加高地的平民穿它做衣服,也用于军队。沿海地区则多穿较轻的棉质衣物。\n\n与它相对的是 **cumbi(qompi)**。这种细密的挂毯式织物用羊驼、小羊驼和骆马(vicuña)的纤维织成,只供皇帝、贵族和宗教场合使用,由国家作坊里的专业织工制作。\n\n各来源在细节上略有出入。有的说 awasca 是用于毯子和地毯的粗羊驼毛或美洲驼毛织物,有的则把毯子归为更粗的 chusi。我只搜了一次,没有核实线密度之类的具体数据。\n\nSources:\n- [Inca Textiles – World History Encyclopedia](https://www.worldhistory.org/article/791/inca-textiles/.md)\n- [Textile arts of the Indigenous peoples of the Americas – Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Textile_arts_of_the_Indigenous_peoples_of_the_Americas)\n- [Inca Cumbi: Precolumbian Tapestry Traditions](https://scalar.usc.edu/works/andean-tapestry/inca-cumbi)\n- [Andean textiles – Textiles Cusco](https://www.textilescusco.org/andean-textiles)"}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 1039, "cache_read_input_tokens": 4313, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 1039}, "output_tokens": 3, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-20T23:45:01.553Z", "request_id": "req_011CgENufuc519SXg8pARBPP"}
